so I want to talk about attack on Titan  and lost really I'm going to be talking  about attack on Titan even though  largely I'm going to be talking about  lost well let's let's now let's just  roll back to the beating okay so I  started watching attack on Titan and I  made it about half way through the first  season when my like BS meter started  like going off like a klaxon something  something was wrong something was off so  I decided that it was time and I just i  Doan - all the spoilers  I read you know I started pulling up  summaries all the way through - like  everything that's being published now in  the manga and you know it's like okay  what's going on what's the meta plot  what do we know what do we not know and  I noticed a pattern and now we're going  to talk about lost so lost fell victim  to a common problem with broadcast  television which is that the serial  content for broadcast television is  generally intended to go on for as long  as humanly possible  you make a series and the goal at  minimum is well six seasons in a movie  but realistically like everyone is kind  of hoping to be the next law in order or  the next simpsons with 20-plus seasons  or something like mash with decades of  syndication but that's just not feasible  and it's not really sustainable because  the narrative economy is largely based  on novelty being able to generate new  episodes being able to generate new  ideas being able to consistently engage  the audience in a way that is both  familiar and surprising that need  essentially in this economic metaphor  can lead to unsustainable inflation and  of course the tipping moment is usually  referred to as jumping the shark loss  suffered dramatically as a result of  this need to keep things going the show  was initially pitched to the audience as  a mystery and the need for mysteries the  constantly inflating need for new  mysteries bloated the series in a  completely unsustainable way to the  point that by the time we were in like  the fifth and sixth season they were  trying to wrap stuff up and but there  was it was impossible at that point  there was no way that they were going to  be able to wrap stuff up because there  was too many hanging threads  there were there were mysteries that  they had cooked up in season one with no  clue how they were ever going to resolve  them and they just never came back to  them ever ever and they were just they  were left completely unresolved and a  lot of fans did not react positively to  that everything I'm saying here I'm  saying about lost but I'm actually  saying it about attack on Titan these  problems are not just a problem with the  show needing to Pat itself out in order  to avoid catching up with the manga  these are actually problems that are  core to the manga itself because the  manga is also a serial production now  this is not a condemnation inventing  things by the seat of your pants is not  a bad way of doing things it's just a  chaotic and unorganized way of doing  things for example the Mongoose a Maki  was also being developed by the  seat-of-the-pants in a largely  serialized self-perpetuating way however  as it neared the end of its run Ito is  able to pull all of those together into  a very tight very cohesive ending that  really really resonates it's a great  ending fantastic ending and fantastic  resolution to the series so just because  you're kind of just making stuff up as  you go that doesn't mean you're doomed  it does however mean that you're  potentially at risk of falling victim to  the same kind of ending as lost where  you have created so many twists and so  many mysteries that your entire world is  now just one giant pile of self  contradictory information and there is  no way to resolve it in any satisfactory  way
 

